"""Authored source for IAT New Test 9. Each row is individually reviewed."""
from pathlib import Path
import csv
rows=[]
def add(n, fmt, stem, choices, key, why, wrong, point, work=None):
    e=f'Answer: ({key}) {choices["ABCD".index(key)]}.\n'
    if work:
        e+=f'Given: {work[0]}\nFormula/Principle: {work[1]}\nStep 1: {work[2]}\n'
        if len(work)>4:e+=f'Step 2: {work[3]}\n'
    if n>=31:
        assert work and len(work)>=4
        e+='Worked derivation: '+work[-1]+'\n'
    e+=f'Why: {why}\nWhy not the others: {wrong}\nKey point: {point}'
    rows.append([n,fmt,stem,*choices,key,e])
# Biology
add(1,'conceptual','A fern bears one type of spore, and a free-living prothallus develops from it. Which conclusion follows?', ['It is homosporous; its prothallus is the gametophyte','It is heterosporous; the prothallus is sporophyte','Its spores are diploid gametes','Its prothallus makes spores by meiosis'],'A','A single spore class defines homospory. Germination gives the haploid prothallus bearing gametangia; the spore-bearing leafy fern is the sporophyte. This identifies the free-living stage from its origin and reproductive structures rather than from its size.','(B) invents two spore classes and mislabels the generation; (C) spores are products of meiosis, not gametes; (D) spore meiosis belongs to the sporophyte sporangium.','Track ploidy and generation separately.')
add(2,'conceptual','A floral formula records K(5) C(5) A5 G̲(2), with the underline denoting a superior ovary. Which interpretation is consistent?', ['Free sepals and two free carpels','Five fused sepals, five fused petals and a bicarpellary syncarpous superior ovary','An inferior ovary and ten stamens','A monocarpellary ovary with free petals'],'B','Parentheses indicate fusion within a whorl: K(5), C(5), and G(2) are fused groups. A5 means five stamens; the stated convention marks the ovary superior.','(A) ignores the parentheses; (C) changes both ovary position and stamen count; (D) contradicts petal and carpel fusion.','Read the symbols and position convention independently.')
add(3,'diagram','The enzyme-rate figure compares an uninhibited curve with the curve under inhibitor X. Which inhibition mechanism best fits both Km and Vmax?', ['Irreversible loss of enzyme','Uncompetitive inhibition lowering both parameters','Competitive inhibition that can be overcome by high substrate','Activation of catalytic turnover'],'C','The same asymptotic Vmax means sufficiently abundant substrate restores the maximal rate, while a larger substrate concentration is needed for half-maximal rate. Competition for the active site has this pattern.','(A) usually lowers functional enzyme and Vmax; (B) changes both Km and Vmax downward; (D) would increase maximal output rather than preserve it.','Compare both Km and Vmax, not a single rate.')
add(4,'diagram','In the leaf cross-section, bundle-sheath cells form a chloroplast-rich ring around each vascular bundle and mesophyll surrounds the ring. Which conclusion best fits?', ['CAM storage is certain','Only C3 fixation can occur','The bundle sheath lacks chloroplasts','Kranz anatomy supports spatial C4 carbon concentration'],'D','The pictured concentric organisation is Kranz anatomy. Initial fixation in mesophyll can be followed by CO₂ release near RuBisCO in bundle sheath, reducing oxygenation under suitable conditions.','(A) CAM relies on temporal rather than this spatial separation; (B) conflicts with the usual C4 interpretation; (C) contradicts the drawn chloroplasts.','Anatomy supports a pathway inference, not a measured rate.')
add(5,'experiment','A postsynaptic neuron responds to externally applied transmitter but not to a presynaptic action potential. Its axon still conducts when directly stimulated. What is the most localised defect?', ['Transmitter release at the presynaptic terminal','Loss of all postsynaptic receptors','Failure of axonal Na⁺ channels throughout the postsynaptic neuron','Inability of the transmitter to bind the intact receptor'],'A','Direct transmitter application verifies that postsynaptic receptor and downstream excitability can work. Direct axon stimulation verifies conduction. The missing response only when presynaptic input is used points to release or delivery from that terminal.','(B) and (D) conflict with the direct application response; (C) conflicts with direct axonal conduction.','Use bypass experiments to locate a failed step.')
add(6,'conceptual','After fertilisation in an angiosperm, what do an ovule and an ovary generally become, respectively?', ['Embryo and endosperm','Seed and fruit','Fruit and seed','Seed coat and pollen grain'],'B','The fertilised ovule matures as a seed containing the embryo; the enclosing ovary generally develops into the fruit. Integuments contribute the seed coat, but that is only one part of the seed.','(A) names contents rather than the enclosing structures; (C) reverses the two; (D) substitutes a substructure and an unrelated male gametophyte.','Follow the whole structures through maturation.')
add(7,'conceptual','An X-linked recessive disorder affects a father and not his partner, who is homozygous normal. What is expected among their children?', ['Every son affected','Half of sons affected','Every daughter a carrier and every son unaffected','Every daughter affected'],'C','The father gives his Y to sons and affected X to daughters. The mother supplies a normal X to each child; daughters are heterozygous carriers and sons receive their only X from her.','(A) and (B) incorrectly send the paternal X to sons; (D) overlooks the normal maternal X.','Separate paternal transmission by child sex.')
add(8,'conceptual','A cell culture loses p53 function. Following DNA damage it continues through G1/S and accumulates mutations. What best explains its greater cancer risk?', ['It automatically gains a new chromosome each division','Repair becomes unnecessary','Mitosis becomes meiosis','The damage checkpoint fails to pause or eliminate compromised cells'],'D','p53 supports arrest and downstream repair or apoptosis responses after damage. Without that restraint, damaged genomes enter replication and pass variants to descendants, increasing opportunity for malignant selection.','(A) is not automatic; (B) repair remains needed; (C) changes the division type without support.','A failed checkpoint permits propagation of damage.')
add(9,'experiment','A plasmid has ampicillin resistance and a disrupted tetracycline-resistance gene when insert is present. Transformants first grow on ampicillin. What screen selects likely recombinants?', ['Replica plate and retain colonies that fail to grow on tetracycline','Keep only colonies growing on both drugs','Keep cells that never grew on ampicillin','Discard all ampicillin-resistant colonies'],'A','Ampicillin growth selects plasmid-containing cells. A correctly inserted fragment disables tetracycline resistance, so ampicillin-positive but tetracycline-negative colonies are candidate recombinants; verify insert by a separate assay.','(B) favours intact vector; (C) lacks evidence of plasmid uptake; (D) discards the selected transformants.','Selection and insertional screening answer different questions.')
add(10,'data','In an energy-flow measurement, producers fix 10,000 kJ; 2,000 kJ enters herbivores, which lose 1,200 kJ as heat and pass 300 kJ to predators. How much herbivore energy enters detritus?', ['300 kJ','500 kJ','800 kJ','1,500 kJ'],'B','Conservation across the herbivore box gives 2,000 kJ in minus 1,200 kJ heat minus 300 kJ predator transfer, leaving 500 kJ for detritus. This bookkeeping does not assume an arbitrary 10% rule.','(A) repeats predator transfer; (C) subtracts only heat; (D) subtracts only detrital or trophic routes incorrectly.','Account for every output of the same compartment.',('Input 2000 kJ and two known outputs.','Energy balance: detritus = input − heat − predation.','2000−1200−300=500 kJ.'))
add(11,'conceptual','A replication fork advances right. The upper template runs 3′→5′ toward the fork, and its new daughter extends continuously toward the fork. Which statement is correct?', ['New DNA grows 3′→5′','Both daughters extend continuously in the fork direction','The upper daughter is leading and grows 5′→3′','The lower daughter must be synthesized 3′→5′'],'C','DNA polymerase extends the 3′ end, giving synthesis 5′→3′. Reading the upper template 3′→5′ toward the advancing fork allows continuous leading-strand synthesis in that direction; the other template requires fragments.','(A) reverses polymerase direction; (B) ignores antiparallel templates; (D) reverses synthesis direction on the lagging strand.','The 5′→3′ synthesis rule applies to both daughters.')
add(12,'data','In the lac-operon table, strain X expresses lacZ without lactose but shuts off in glucose; strain Y responds normally to both conditions. X has a repressor that cannot bind operator but intact glucose control. What explains X?', ['A defective promoter preventing all transcription','An enzyme unable to cleave lactose','A permanently inactive polymerase','Constitutive operator-level derepression with catabolite repression retained'],'D','A repressor that cannot occupy operator cannot shut the operon off in the absence of inducer. The intact glucose control still lowers activation under glucose, explaining both observed features.','(A) and (C) predict no expression; (B) concerns substrate processing rather than operator occupancy and does not explain constitutive transcription.','Two regulatory layers can act independently.')
add(13,'conceptual','A time-series measurement shows that a rise in circulating thyroid hormone is followed by a fall in TSH while external conditions are unchanged. Which feedback link is supported?', ['Thyroid hormone negatively feeds back on pituitary TSH output','TSH suppresses thyroid hormone irreversibly','Thyroid hormone is made by the pituitary','The thyroid axis has only positive feedback'],'A','An increase in the downstream hormone followed by a reduced upstream drive under otherwise stable conditions is the expected sign of negative feedback. The graph alone does not identify every molecular intermediate.','(B) reverses the observed causal direction; (C) misplaces the hormone source; (D) predicts TSH increasing rather than falling.','Use direction and sequence of change to identify feedback sign.')
add(14,'experiment','A cell-free translation mix has intact mRNA, ribosomes and charged tRNAs. A drug blocks movement of the ribosome by one codon after peptide-bond formation. Which stage directly fails?', ['Initiation of transcription','Elongation translocation','Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase charging','DNA ligation'],'B','Peptide bond formation can occur, so the immediate blocked step is translocation during elongation, when mRNA/tRNAs shift relative to the ribosome to expose the next codon.','(A) is upstream and mRNA is already present; (C) is excluded by charged tRNAs and timing; (D) is unrelated to peptide synthesis.','Map the inhibited movement to its translation stage.')
add(15,'numerical','For the same habitat type, species richness follows S=cA^0.25. If sampled area rises by a factor of 16, by what factor does expected richness rise?', ['4', '8', '16', '2'],'D','The species-area law uses a sublinear exponent, so the richness ratio is 16^0.25=2, not the area ratio. c cancels for two areas in the same comparison.','(B) treats the exponent as one half; (C) uses three quarters; (D) assumes direct proportionality.','Take the ratio before substituting the area factor.',('S=cA^0.25 and A₂/A₁=16.','S₂/S₁=(A₂/A₁)^0.25.','16^(1/4)=2.'))
# Chemistry
add(16,'conceptual','The H–O–H angle is about 104.5°, below the tetrahedral ideal. Which electron-pair explanation is best?', ['No lone pair on oxygen','Three bonding pairs on oxygen','Two lone pairs exert stronger repulsion than bonding pairs and compress the angle','The molecule is linear but rotates rapidly'],'C','Oxygen has two O–H bond pairs and two lone pairs in approximately tetrahedral electron-pair geometry. Lone-pair repulsions push bonding pairs closer, reducing the observed bond angle.','(A) omits two lone pairs; (B) counts bonds incorrectly; (D) contradicts bent molecular geometry.','Distinguish electron-pair geometry from molecular shape.')
add(17,'diagram','Use the coordination sketch and its donor-site labels to find the metal oxidation state and coordination number.', ['+2 and 4','+2 and 6','+3 and 4','+3 and 6'],'D','Let Co oxidation state be x. Two neutral en and two Cl⁻ give x−2=+1, so x=+3. Each en donates two sites (four total) and two chlorides one each, giving coordination number six.','(A) and (B) miscount charge; (C) counts ligand molecules rather than donor atoms.','Charge and donor count require separate calculations.')
add(18,'conceptual','Which ion is d⁰ and hence lacks d–d transitions within its own d subshell?', ['Sc³⁺','Ti³⁺','V³⁺','Cr³⁺'],'A','Neutral Sc is [Ar]3d¹4s²; removal of three electrons gives [Ar]3d⁰. With no d electron, a d–d excitation is unavailable, though other colour mechanisms can exist in compounds.','(B) Ti³⁺ is d¹; (C) V³⁺ is d²; (D) Cr³⁺ is d³.','Compute the ionic d count before invoking colour.')
add(19,'numerical','A hydrogen atom emits a photon in the 3→2 transition. If Eₙ=−13.6/n² eV and hc=1240 eV·nm, its wavelength is closest to?', ['486 nm','656 nm','434 nm','122 nm'],'B','The energy gap is 13.6(1/4−1/9)=1.889 eV, so λ=1240/1.889≈656 nm. The emitted photon energy is the positive difference of level energies. Since wavelength varies inversely with energy, choosing a larger transition gap would give a shorter line.','(A) and (C) correspond to different Balmer transitions; (D) is much shorter wavelength and requires a larger gap.','Use energy difference, then convert by hc/E.',('Hydrogen levels n=3 and 2.','ΔE=13.6(1/2²−1/3²) eV; λ=hc/ΔE.','ΔE=1.889 eV.','λ≈1240/1.889=656 nm.'))
add(20,'conceptual','Why can the first ionisation energy of oxygen be lower than that of nitrogen despite oxygen being to its right in period 2?', ['Oxygen has a completely empty 2p shell','Nitrogen has a paired 2p orbital','Oxygen has a paired 2p electron; nitrogen has a more stable half-filled 2p³ arrangement','Nitrogen has a larger principal quantum number'],'C','Nitrogen 2p³ puts one electron in each p orbital; oxygen 2p⁴ introduces a pair. Repulsion in that pair eases removal relative to the half-filled nitrogen configuration, overcoming the simple across-period trend locally.','(A) reverses configuration; (B) gives the pair to nitrogen; (D) both occupy n=2.','Local subshell stability modifies periodic trends.')
add(21,'numerical','A+B→C is 1:1. Starting with 0.10 mol A and 0.16 mol B, C is isolated at 80% yield. All isolated C then reacts by 2C→D at 50% yield. How many moles of D are isolated?', ['0.020','0.040','0.050','0.080'],'A','A limits the first step at 0.10 mol theoretical C; 80% isolation gives 0.080 mol C. The second stoichiometry gives 0.040 mol theoretical D and 50% of this is 0.020 mol. Apply each yield to its own stage.','(B) omits second yield; (C) applies yield incorrectly before 2:1 conversion; (D) reports isolated C as D.','Carry isolated intermediate to the next reaction.',('A=0.10 mol, B=0.16 mol; yields 80%, then 50%.','n(C)=0.80 min(nA,nB); n(D)=0.50 n(C)/2.','n(C)=0.080 mol.','n(D)=0.020 mol.'))
add(22,'conceptual','Compare p-nitrobenzoic acid and p-methoxybenzoic acid in water. Which is generally more acidic, and why?', ['Methoxy acid: +M donation stabilises its anion','Nitro acid: electron withdrawal stabilises its conjugate base','Equal: para groups cannot affect resonance','Methoxy acid: inductive donation alone dominates'],'B','The para nitro group withdraws electron density through resonance and induction, stabilising the negatively charged benzoate relative to the acid. Para methoxy donates through resonance and tends to oppose this stabilisation.','(A) calls electron donation stabilising for an anion; (C) ignores conjugation through the ring; (D) reverses the key para resonance effect.','Judge acidity by relative conjugate-base stability.')
add(23,'conceptual','Which single compound can undergo self-aldol addition under basic conditions?', ['Benzaldehyde','Formaldehyde','Propanal','Benzophenone'],'C','Propanal has an alpha carbon bearing hydrogens, so enolate formation and attack on another propanal molecule are possible. The listed aromatic or one-carbon carbonyl compounds lack alpha hydrogen in the needed position.','(A) benzaldehyde lacks an alpha carbon; (B) formaldehyde lacks one; (D) benzophenone has phenyl groups directly attached to carbonyl carbon, without alpha hydrogen.','Check for at least one alpha hydrogen on the enolate donor.')
add(24,'conceptual','Which compound has the strongest acidity among the listed phenols?', ['p-Methoxyphenol','Phenol','p-Methylphenol','p-Nitrophenol'],'D','A para nitro substituent withdraws electron density and stabilises phenoxide by resonance and induction. Methoxy and methyl are electron donating in the relevant comparison, while phenol lacks substituent stabilisation.','(A) methoxy resonance donation destabilises phenoxide; (B) lacks nitro stabilisation; (C) methyl donation does not stabilise the anion.','Compare resonance stabilisation of the conjugate base.')
add(25,'experiment','An unknown amine reacts with benzenesulfonyl chloride in alkali to give a sulfonamide insoluble in alkali; it does not dissolve after acidification. Which class is most consistent?', ['Secondary amine','Primary amine','Tertiary amine','Ammonium salt without an amine'],'A','A secondary amine forms a sulfonamide with no N–H remaining, so it does not form the soluble anion in alkali. A primary sulfonamide retains an acidic N–H and dissolves in alkali; a tertiary amine lacks the N–H needed to form this sulfonamide.','(B) product should dissolve in base; (C) cannot furnish the described sulfonamide; (D) does not explain the reaction product.','Hinsberg solubility depends on residual sulfonamide N–H.')
add(26,'conceptual','At a pH far above its isoelectric point, a simple amino acid without an ionisable side chain predominantly has what net charge?', ['+2','−1','0','+1'],'B','High pH deprotonates the carboxyl to COO⁻ and the ammonium group to neutral NH₂, giving a net −1. This describes the predominant form sufficiently above both relevant pKa values.','(A) overcounts positive charge; (C) is the zwitterion near its isoelectric region; (D) is favoured at strongly acidic pH.','Compare pH with protonation equilibria of both groups.')
add(27,'diagram','Using the pure-component and observed pressures shown in the figure at its marked liquid composition, what deviation occurs?', ['Negative, 15 kPa','Zero, ideal 85 kPa','Positive, 15 kPa','Positive, 30 kPa'],'C','Raoult ideal pressure at equimolar composition is 0.5(100)+0.5(40)=70 kPa. Observed pressure exceeds ideal by 15 kPa, the signature of positive deviation from Raoult’s law.','(A) reverses sign; (B) treats observed as ideal; (D) computes the magnitude incorrectly.','Compare observed pressure to weighted pure-component pressures.',('xA=xB=0.50; p*A=100, p*B=40, observed=85 kPa.','pideal=xAp*A+xBp*B.','pideal=70 kPa.','85−70=+15 kPa.'))
add(28,'numerical','A concentration cell Cu|Cu²⁺(0.010 M)||Cu²⁺(1.0 M)|Cu operates at 298 K. With E° identical and 0.0591 V in the Nernst factor, what is Ecell?', ['0.0296 V','0.0591 V','0.1182 V','0 V'],'B','The higher-concentration Cu²⁺ half-cell is reduced, the lower is oxidised. E=(0.0591/2)log(1/0.010)=0.0591 V; standard potentials cancel. The net cell reaction transfers copper ions from the concentrated side toward the dilute side, reducing the concentration difference.','(A) misses log ratio 100; (C) omits electron count two; (D) overlooks concentration difference.','Use concentration ratio and n=2.',('Cu²⁺ concentrations differ by factor 100.','E=(0.0591/n) log(cathode/anode), n=2.','E=0.02955×2=0.0591 V.'))
add(29,'numerical','For a reaction with Ea=50 kJ mol⁻¹, what is approximately k(310 K)/k(300 K)? Take R=8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.', ['1.91', '1.09', '1.48', '4.8'],'A','The two-temperature Arrhenius ratio has ln(k₂/k₁)=(Ea/R)(1/T₁−1/T₂)≈0.647, hence exp(0.647)≈1.91. The factor is dimensionless and greater than one as temperature rises. Converting activation energy to joules is necessary because the stated gas constant uses joules.','(B) underestimates exponential sensitivity; (C) uses a smaller exponent; (D) exceeds the calculated factor.','Work with reciprocal absolute temperatures.',('T₁=300 K, T₂=310 K, Ea=50000 J mol⁻¹.','ln(k₂/k₁)=Ea/R(1/T₁−1/T₂).','Exponent ≈0.647.','Ratio ≈e^0.647=1.91.'))
add(30,'conceptual','For N₂+3H₂⇌2NH₃ at fixed T, the initial quotient Q is less than K. What is the immediate spontaneous direction?', ['Reverse, because ammonia is a product','No change, because T is fixed','Both directions equally','Forward until Q approaches K'],'D','Q<K means the present products-to-reactants activity ratio is below its equilibrium value. Forward reaction increases product relative to reactants until the equilibrium condition is approached.','(A) uses product identity instead of Q; (B) fixed T fixes K but not Q; (C) equilibrium is the condition Q=K, not Q<K.','Compare reaction quotient with equilibrium constant.')
# Mathematics: all solutions include a displayed derivation and targeted distractor analysis.
add(31,'numerical','Let A be invertible 3×3 with det(A)=3. Form B by adding twice row 1 to row 2, then multiplying row 3 by −2. What is det(adj B)?',['−36','−6','36','6'],'C','The row addition preserves determinant and the scaling makes det B=−6. For any invertible 3×3 matrix, det(adj B)=(det B)², giving 36; the sign disappears only at the second operation.','(A) retains the determinant sign after squaring; (B) stops at det B; (D) takes the magnitude of det B but omits the adjugate identity.','Track row effects, then apply the dimension-dependent adjugate identity.',('A is 3×3, det A=3.','det B=−2 det A; det(adj B)=(det B)^(3−1).','det B=−6.',r'\[\det(\operatorname{adj}B)=(\det B)^2=(-2\cdot3)^2=36.\]'))
add(32,'numerical','On a square grid, how many right/up paths go from (0,0) to (3,3) without passing through (1,1)?',['12', '10', '6', '8'],'D','There are 6 choose 3 =20 paths overall. A path through (1,1) has 2 choose 1 ways to reach it and 4 choose 2 ways afterward, 12 forbidden paths. Subtract to get 8; giving the selected 8 valid paths.','(A) counts forbidden paths; (B) miscounts the complement; (C) understates valid paths.','Use product counting through the forbidden point.',('Three R and three U steps; forbidden point (1,1).','Nallowed=C(6,3)−C(2,1)C(4,2).','20−2·6=8.',r'\[N=\binom63-\binom21\binom42=20-12=8.\]'))
add(33,'conceptual','For real x, which inputs make ln(x²−2) defined if this expression is obtained by f(g(x)), with f(t)=ln(t−1) and g(x)=x²−1?',['(−∞,−√2)∪(√2,∞)','[−√2,√2]','All nonzero x','(−∞,−1)∪(1,∞)'],'A','Composition gives ln(x²−2), whose argument must be strictly positive. Solving x²>2 gives two open rays; equality is excluded because ln 0 is undefined.','(B) reverses the inequality and includes endpoints; (C) permits arguments below zero; (D) uses the domain of g rather than the composed logarithm.','Apply the outer logarithm restriction after composition.',('f(g(x))=ln(x²−2).','Logarithm argument x²−2>0.','x<−√2 or x>√2.',r'\[x^2-2>0\iff x\in(-\infty,-\sqrt2)\cup(\sqrt2,\infty).\]'))
add(34,'numerical','In the Argand plane, what locus satisfies |z−(1+i)|=|z−(3−i)|?',['x+y=2','x−y=2','x+y=1','x−y=1'],'B','For z=x+iy, equate squared distances to (1,1) and (3,−1). Simplification gives −4x+4y+8=0, hence x−y=2. The locus is the perpendicular bisector of the two points.','(A) has wrong orientation; (C) changes the constant; (D) is a shifted parallel line.','Equidistance becomes a linear equation after squares cancel.',('Points (1,1) and (3,−1).','(x−1)²+(y−1)²=(x−3)²+(y+1)².','4x−4y−8=0.',r'\[(x-1)^2+(y-1)^2=(x-3)^2+(y+1)^2\iff x-y=2.\]'))
add(35,'numerical','Evaluate ∫₀^π x sin x dx.',['π/2','2π','π','0'],'C','Integration by parts with u=x and dv=sin x dx gives −x cos x+sin x. At π this is π, at zero it is zero.','(A) halves the boundary term; (B) doubles it; (D) overlooks the nonzero endpoint contribution.','Keep the boundary term in integration by parts.',('Integral from 0 to π.','∫x sin x dx=−x cos x+sin x.','Upper endpoint π; lower endpoint 0.',r'\[\int_0^\pi x\sin x\,dx=[-x\cos x+\sin x]_0^\pi=\pi.\]'))
add(36,'numerical','Project the point P=(1,2) orthogonally onto the oblique line 3x+4y−10=0 in the plane. Which ordered pair is obtained?',['(4/5,9/5)','(0,5/2)','(1,7/4)','(22/25,46/25)'],'D','The signed line expression at P is 3+8−10=1. Subtract 1/(3²+4²) times the normal vector (3,4), yielding (22/25,46/25), the selected foot.','(A) is off the projection; (B) lies elsewhere on the line; (C) is not a normal displacement.','Project along the oblique line normal.',('P=(1,2), normal=(3,4), signed offset=1.','H=P−[(3·1+4·2−10)/25](3,4).','H=(22/25,46/25).',r'\[H=(1,2)-\frac1{25}(3,4)=\left(\frac{22}{25},\frac{46}{25}\right).\]'))
add(37,'numerical','For f(x)=ax+b if x<1, and x² if x≥1, which (a,b) make f differentiable at x=1?',['(2,−1)','(1,0)','(2,1)','(−2,3)'],'A','Continuity first requires a+b=1. Equality of one-sided derivatives requires a=2, so b=−1. Both conditions are necessary; matching only value is insufficient.','(B) is continuous but slopes differ; (C) has matching derivative but a+b≠1; (D) is continuous but left slope −2.','Check value and slope at the joining point.',('Piecewise join at x=1.','Continuity a+b=1; derivative match a=2.','b=−1.',r'\[a+b=1,\quad a=2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad(a,b)=(2,-1).\]'))
add(38,'numerical','Two groups each have 10 values with means 2 and 4 and population variances 1 and 1. What is the variance of all 20 values?',['1','2','3','4'],'B','The combined mean is 3. Within-group variance contributes 1; the two group means differ from 3 by ±1, adding between-group variance 1. Total population variance is 2.','(A) ignores between-group spread; (C) adds a mean gap without squaring/weighting correctly; (D) treats the full mean gap as a deviation.','Combine within and between variance.',('Equal group sizes, means 2 and 4, variances 1 each.','V=average[Vgroup+(μgroup−μ)²].','μ=3; V=(1+1+1+1)/2=2.',r'\[\mu=3,\qquad V=\tfrac12[1+(2-3)^2+1+(4-3)^2]=2.\]'))
add(39,'numerical','Find a such that lim(x→0) (sin(ax)−2x)/x³ exists as a finite real number.',['0','1','2','3'],'C','Expand sin(ax)=ax−a³x³/6+higher terms. The coefficient of x must vanish for the quotient to avoid x⁻² divergence, so a=2. The resulting finite limit is −8/6.','(A), (B) and (D) leave a nonzero (a−2)x term, producing divergence upon division by x³.','Cancel lower-order terms before taking a higher-order limit.',('Limit of [sin(ax)−2x]/x³.','sin(ax)=ax−a³x³/6+O(x⁵).','Finite iff a−2=0.',r'\[\frac{\sin(ax)-2x}{x^3}=\frac{a-2}{x^2}-\frac{a^3}{6}+O(x^2);\quad a=2.\]'))
add(40,'numerical','Urn U₁ is chosen with probability 1/3 and has 2 red, 1 blue; U₂ with probability 2/3 has 1 red, 3 blue. After observing red, what is P(U₁|red)?',['1/3','2/3','1/2','4/7'],'D','The red joint probabilities are (1/3)(2/3)=2/9 and (2/3)(1/4)=1/6. Their sum is 7/18, so posterior U₁ is (2/9)/(7/18)=4/7. The observation favours U₁ despite its smaller prior because red is substantially more likely there.','(A) repeats the prior; (B) uses U₁ likelihood alone; (C) ignores unequal priors and urn compositions.','Normalise joint probabilities after the observation.',('P(U₁)=1/3, P(R|U₁)=2/3; P(U₂)=2/3, P(R|U₂)=1/4.','Bayes P(U₁|R)=P(U₁)P(R|U₁)/P(R).','P(R)=2/9+1/6=7/18.',r'\[P(U_1\mid R)=\frac{(1/3)(2/3)}{(1/3)(2/3)+(2/3)(1/4)}=\frac47.\]'))
add(41,'numerical','If tan θ=1/2 with θ acute, find tan(2θ).',['4/3','3/4','1','2'],'A','Apply tan 2θ=2t/(1−t²) with t=1/2: numerator 1, denominator 3/4, so 4/3. The acute condition fixes a consistent quadrant but the algebra already determines the value.','(B) inverts the result; (C) ignores the denominator; (D) doubles t incorrectly.','Use the full double-angle denominator.',('tan θ=1/2.','tan 2θ=2tanθ/(1−tan²θ).','1/(1−1/4)=4/3.',r'\[\tan2\theta=\frac{2(1/2)}{1-(1/2)^2}=\frac43.\]'))
add(42,'numerical','For x>0 and y>0, solve dy/dx=(x²+y²)/(xy) with y(1)=1. What is y(e)?',['e√2','e√3','2e','3e'],'B','This homogeneous differential equation becomes separable after putting y=xv. The initial point selects the positive square-root branch, which matters when evaluating y at e.','(A) omits the initial-value contribution; (C) uses an incorrect constant; (D) squares the required root.','Substitute y=xv, integrate, then apply the positive branch.',('For x,y>0, y′=x/y+y/x.','Set y=xv; then v+xv′=1/v+v.','v dv=dx/x, so v²=2ln x+C; v(1)=1 fixes C=1.',r'\[y=x\sqrt{1+2\ln x},\qquad y(e)=e\sqrt3.\]'))
add(43,'numerical','For which real k are both roots of x²−2kx+(k+3)=0 real and in the open interval (0,3)?',['k≥(1+√13)/2','(1+√13)/2<k≤12/5','(1+√13)/2≤k<12/5','k>12/5'],'C','Discriminant nonnegative and positive roots force k≥(1+√13)/2. To keep both roots below 3, the vertex k must lie below 3 and the polynomial at x=3 must be positive: 12−5k>0, so k<12/5. At the lower end the repeated root lies inside (0,3), while the upper end places a root at excluded x=3.','(A) includes arbitrarily large k; (B) wrongly excludes the valid repeated root and includes the endpoint 3; (D) makes the polynomial at 3 negative.','Combine discriminant, vertex position, endpoint signs and strict boundaries.',('Polynomial has sum 2k, product k+3, vertex x=k.','Δ=4(k²−k−3)≥0; positive branch k≥(1+√13)/2.','At x=3, p(3)=12−5k>0 and k<3.','Hence lower endpoint included, upper excluded.',r'\[k\in\left[\frac{1+\sqrt{13}}2,\frac{12}{5}\right).\]'))
add(44,'conceptual','Every line in the family (1+t)x+(2−t)y=3+t passes through which point?',['(1,1)', '(0,3/2)', '(2,0)', '(5/3,2/3)'],'D','Collect constant and t terms: x+2y−3+t(x−y−1)=0. A common point satisfies both x+2y=3 and x−y=1; solving gives x=5/3,y=2/3, the common point in option D.','(A) fails the t coefficient; (B) fails the second constraint; (C) fails the constant constraint.','Set both polynomial coefficients in t to zero.',('Line family holds for every t.','x+2y−3=0 and x−y−1=0.','Solve simultaneous equations.',r'\[x=y+1,\quad y+1+2y=3\implies (x,y)=(5/3,2/3).\]'))
add(45,'numerical','For which k are (1,2), (3,6), and (k,10) collinear?',['4', '5', '6', '7'],'B','The first two points have slope (6−2)/(3−1)=2. The line through (1,2) is y=2x; setting y=10 gives x=5. This is option B.','(A), (C) and (D) do not satisfy the line equation y=2x at y=10.','Use one line equation and substitute the third point.',('First two points define y=2x.','For y=10, x=10/2.','k=5.',r'\[\frac{6-2}{3-1}=2,\quad y-2=2(x-1)\implies 10=2k\implies k=5.\]'))
# Physics
add(46,'graph','The figure gives the static-friction threshold for a block on a horizontal floor. While the block remains at rest under a horizontal 4 N applied force, what is its friction?', ['4 N opposite the applied force','6 N opposite the force','2 N opposite the force','Zero until sliding'],'A','For a stationary block static friction adjusts to match the applied horizontal force up to its limiting value. At 4 N, below the measured 6 N limit, force balance requires 4 N opposite the applied force.','(B) confuses the maximum with actual friction; (C) leaves unbalanced force; (D) would let the block accelerate before the observed threshold.','Static friction is self-adjusting below its limit.',('Applied 4 N < limiting 6 N.','Horizontal equilibrium: fₛ=Fapplied while at rest.','fₛ=4 N, opposite the force.',r'\[\sum F_x=0\implies f_s=F=4\,\mathrm N<6\,\mathrm N.\]'))
add(47,'numerical','A one-dimensional conservative force is F(x)=x²−4x newtons, with x measured in metres. What is its signed work as a particle moves from x=0 to x=4 m?',['+32/3 J','−32/3 J','0','−16 J'],'B','The force is negative throughout the open interval 0<x<4, so work must be negative. Integrating gives [x³/3−2x²]₀⁴=64/3−32=−32/3 J. The zeros of force at the endpoints do not cancel the negative area accumulated between them.','(A) uses magnitude but loses sign; (C) assumes endpoint zeros imply zero area; (D) replaces the curved force profile by an incorrect constant.','The signed area under F(x) determines work.',('F=x²−4x, 0≤x≤4.','W=∫₀⁴F(x) dx.','W=[x³/3−2x²]₀⁴=−32/3 J.',r'\[W=\int_0^4(x^2-4x)\,dx=\left[\frac{x^3}{3}-2x^2\right]_0^4=-\frac{32}{3}\,\mathrm J.\]'))
add(48,'numerical','At a planet surface r=R, escape speed is v. What minimum launch speed reaches r=4R with zero speed there, ignoring atmosphere and rotation?',['v/2','v/4','√3v/2','3v/4'],'C','Energy conservation requires initial kinetic energy equal to the increase in gravitational potential from −GMm/R to −GMm/(4R). That difference is 3GMm/(4R). As v²=2GM/R, the required speed is √3v/2, below escape speed because the target is finite.','(A) is local escape speed at 4R, a different quantity; (B) assumes inverse distance scaling of launch speed; (D) omits the square root.','Separate local escape speed from finite-height launch energy.',('v²=2GM/R; target radius 4R.','½mu²=GMm(1/R−1/4R).','u²=(3/4)v².',r'\[u=\sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}\left(1-\frac14\right)}=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}v.\]'))
add(49,'numerical','A stretched rod has longitudinal strain +0.002 and Poisson ratio 0.30. What is the transverse strain?',['+0.006','−0.006','+0.0006','−0.0006'],'D','Poisson ratio ν=−εtrans/εlong. Substituting 0.30 and +0.002 gives εtrans=−0.0006: a tensile rod narrows sideways. Both strains are dimensionless; the negative transverse sign records contraction rather than an additional force.','(A) and (B) multiply by ten; (C) misses the opposite sign.','Poisson ratio contains a minus sign.',('ν=0.30, longitudinal strain +0.002.','εtrans=−ν εlong.','−0.30×0.002=−0.0006.',r'\[\varepsilon_{\perp}=-\nu\varepsilon_{\parallel}=-(0.30)(0.002)=-6\times10^{-4}.\]'))
add(50,'numerical','Two identical hot bodies at 80°C and 60°C cool in air at 20°C under the same Newton cooling constant. What is the ratio of their initial cooling-rate magnitudes, hotter to cooler?', ['3:2','2:1','4:3','1:1'],'A','Newton cooling at the initial moment is proportional to the temperature excess over ambient: 80−20=60 K and 60−20=40 K. The rate ratio is 60:40=3:2.','(B) uses absolute temperatures incorrectly; (C) ratios the two object temperatures; (D) ignores different excesses.','Use temperature excess above the common ambient.',('Ambient 20°C, bodies 80°C and 60°C.','|dT/dt|=k(T−Ta).','60/40=3/2.',r'\[\frac{|\dot T_1|}{|\dot T_2|}=\frac{80-20}{60-20}=\frac32.\]'))
add(51,'diagram','The kinetic-theory sketch shows N molecules of mass m in a cube of side L, each with mean-square x-velocity component ⟨cₓ²⟩. What pressure acts on the wall normal to x?', ['Nm⟨cₓ²⟩/L²','Nm⟨cₓ²⟩/L³','Nm⟨cₓ²⟩/(3L³)','Nm⟨cₓ²⟩/(6L³)'],'B','One molecule transfers momentum 2mcₓ at each collision, with collisions separated by 2L/|cₓ|, giving mean force mcₓ²/L. Sum N and divide by wall area L² to obtain Nm⟨cₓ²⟩/L³. A factor 1/3 is needed only when replacing component mean square by total speed mean square.','(A) misses a length; (C) double-counts the isotropy factor for a component already specified; (D) adds another unwarranted factor.','Derive pressure from momentum flux normal to the wall.',('N molecules in cube volume L³.','P=Nm⟨cₓ²⟩/V.','V=L³.',r'\[P=\frac{N(mc_x^2/L)}{L^2}=\frac{Nm\langle c_x^2\rangle}{L^3}.\]'))
add(52,'numerical','For SHM with total mechanical energy E and amplitude A, what is kinetic energy at displacement x=A/2?', ['E/4','E/2','3E/4','E'],'C','The spring potential at displacement x is E(x²/A²). At A/2 it equals E/4; conservation leaves kinetic energy 3E/4. The speed is still nonzero away from the turning points, where kinetic energy would vanish entirely.','(A) gives potential energy; (B) assumes a linear energy split; (D) is kinetic energy only at equilibrium.','Energy varies as displacement squared.',('E=½kA² and x=A/2.','U/E=x²/A², K=E−U.','U=E/4, K=3E/4.',r'\[K=\frac12k(A^2-x^2)=E\left(1-\frac{x^2}{A^2}\right)=\frac34E.\]'))
add(53,'diagram','The before/after diagram shows a charged capacitor isolated from the battery and then filled with the indicated dielectric. What are its new voltage and energy relative to V₀,U₀?', ['3V₀,3U₀','V₀,U₀','V₀/3,3U₀','V₀/3,U₀/3'],'D','Disconnection fixes free charge Q. Insertion raises capacitance threefold, so V=Q/C falls to one third and U=Q²/(2C) also falls to one third. The force drawing dielectric inward supplies the energy difference.','(A) reverses both changes; (B) incorrectly keeps voltage fixed after disconnection; (C) gives the battery-connected energy trend with inconsistent voltage.','Identify the fixed quantity before choosing energy formula.',('Q fixed; Cnew=3C₀.','V=Q/C, U=Q²/(2C).','Vnew=V₀/3; Unew=U₀/3.',r'\[\frac{V_{\rm new}}{V_0}=\frac{C_0}{3C_0}=\frac13,\qquad \frac{U_{\rm new}}{U_0}=\frac{C_0}{3C_0}=\frac13.\]'))
add(54,'numerical','A positive charge moves with velocity v x̂ through B(ŷ+ẑ). Which uniform electric field makes its total Lorentz force zero?',['vB(ŷ−ẑ)','vB(ẑ−ŷ)','vB(ŷ+ẑ)','Zero'],'A','The magnetic part per unit charge is v×B=vB(ẑ−ŷ). Electric and magnetic forces must cancel, so E=−v×B=vB(ŷ−ẑ). This is a force-balance condition, not a magnetic-force-only direction question.','(B) adds to the magnetic force; (C) points partly parallel to B instead of cancelling v×B; (D) leaves magnetic deflection.','Set E+v×B=0 and resolve both components.',('v=v x̂; B=B(ŷ+ẑ); q>0.','F=q(E+v×B)=0.','v×B=vB(ẑ−ŷ).',r'\[\mathbf E=-\mathbf v\times\mathbf B=vB(\hat{\mathbf y}-\hat{\mathbf z}).\]'))
add(55,'numerical','A closed surface encloses charges +2q, −q and +3q. A fourth charge −5q is outside. What is its net electric flux?', ['0','4q/ε₀','−q/ε₀','−5q/ε₀'],'B','Gauss’s law uses only enclosed net charge: 2q−q+3q=4q. The external charge can alter local field distribution on the surface but its total net flux contribution is zero.','(A) misadds charges; (C) includes outside charge in net; (D) reports only the external source.','Flux through a closed surface depends on enclosed charge.',('Enclosed +2q, −q, +3q; outside −5q.','Φ=Qenclosed/ε₀.','Qenclosed=4q.',r'\[\Phi_E=\frac{2q-q+3q}{\varepsilon_0}=\frac{4q}{\varepsilon_0}.\]'))
add(56,'numerical','A resistor network has two identical 6 Ω resistors in parallel between A and B, then a 2 Ω resistor in series from B to C. What is equivalent resistance between A and C?', ['3 Ω','4 Ω','5 Ω','8 Ω'],'C','The equal parallel pair combines as (6×6)/(6+6)=3 Ω. Current then passes through the 2 Ω series resistor, giving 5 Ω overall. The common A–B endpoints establish parallel connection; the single B–C arm carries their combined current.','(A) omits series arm; (B) treats parallel pair as 2 Ω; (D) adds only one 6 Ω branch to 2 Ω.','Reduce nodes and series/parallel stages.',('Two 6 Ω branches A–B; 2 Ω B–C.','RAB=(1/6+1/6)⁻¹; RAC=RAB+2.','3+2=5 Ω.',r'\[R_{AB}=\left(\frac16+\frac16\right)^{-1}=3\,\Omega,\quad R_{AC}=3+2=5\,\Omega.\]'))
add(57,'numerical','An object 30 cm left of a converging lens (f=10 cm) first forms an image; a plane mirror 20 cm right of the lens reflects those rays back through the lens. Where is the final image after the second lens passage?',['5 cm left of lens','10 cm left of lens','15 cm left of lens','50/3 cm left of lens'],'D','On the first pass the lens makes an image 15 cm to its right. The mirror lies 5 cm beyond it and forms a virtual image at x=25 cm behind the mirror. Returning rays approach the lens as if from an object 25 cm to its right; the lens then makes a real image 50/3 cm to its left.','(A) is mirror image distance from mirror, not final location; (B) is focal length; (C) stops at the first lens image.','Track the intermediate image through both optical elements and the return pass.',('First lens object distance 30 cm, f=10 cm; mirror x=20 cm.','First image x=15; mirror image x=25; second pass lens object distance 25.','Second pass 1/v=1/10−1/25=3/50.',r'\[v_1=15\,\mathrm{cm},\quad x_{\rm mirror\ image}=25\,\mathrm{cm},\quad v_2=\left(\frac1{10}-\frac1{25}\right)^{-1}=\frac{50}{3}\,\mathrm{cm}\text{ left}.\]'))
add(58,'conceptual','At fixed frequency above threshold, light intensity incident on a photocathode doubles while collector voltage is high enough to saturate. What happens to saturation photocurrent and stopping potential?', ['Current doubles; stopping potential unchanged','Both double','Current unchanged; potential doubles','Both unchanged'],'A','Twice the photon flux ejects approximately twice as many photoelectrons per time, raising saturated current. Maximum electron kinetic energy depends on photon frequency, unchanged here, so stopping potential stays fixed.','(B) and (C) incorrectly tie stopping potential to intensity; (D) overlooks the larger electron count.','Intensity controls count; frequency controls maximum energy.',('Frequency fixed, intensity doubled.','Isat∝photon flux; eVstop=hf−φ.','Isat doubles, Vstop unchanged.',r'\[I_{\rm new}=2I_{\rm sat},\qquad eV_{\rm new}=hf-\phi=eV_{\rm stop}.\]'))
add(59,'diagram','The hydrogen-level figure marks n=4,3,2,1. Which downward transition has the longest emitted wavelength among 4→3, 4→2, 3→2, 2→1?', ['4→2','3→2','4→3','2→1'],'C','Longest wavelength means smallest emitted energy. Hydrogen gaps shrink among high neighbouring levels: 4→3 has 13.6(1/9−1/16) eV, less than the other displayed drops.','(A) spans two levels and is larger; (B) has a larger adjacent gap lower in the ladder; (D) has the largest of these gaps.','Compare energy gaps, then invert for wavelength.',('Eₙ=−13.6/n² eV.','ΔE=13.6(1/nlow²−1/nhigh²), λ=hc/ΔE.','4→3 has smallest ΔE.',r'\[\Delta E_{4\to3}=13.6\left(\frac19-\frac1{16}\right)<\Delta E_{3\to2},\Delta E_{4\to2},\Delta E_{2\to1}.\]'))
add(60,'conceptual','Two nearby coils have corresponding dotted ends. The left coil current toward its marked dot decreases. What is the initial induced current direction at the marked dot of the right coil?', ['Out of the right dot','Zero because coils do not touch','Alternates randomly','Into the right dot'],'D','By the dot convention, currents entering corresponding dotted ends produce fluxes of the same reference sign. A decreasing left-dot entering current reduces positive flux in the right coil, and Lenz’s law drives a current entering its dotted end to oppose that reduction.','(A) would reinforce the flux decrease; (B) neglects magnetic coupling; (C) ignores the specified monotonic change.','Choose a reference winding direction and oppose flux change.',('Same dot orientation; left-dot entering current decreases.','Lenz law: induced right current restores declining linked flux.','It enters the right dot.',r'\[\mathcal E_2=-M\frac{dI_1}{dt}>0\quad\text{in the chosen dot-entering reference direction.}\]'))

if __name__=='__main__':
    assert len(rows)==60, len(rows)
    base=Path(__file__).parent
    for sec, lo, hi in [('b',1,15),('c',16,30),('m',31,45),('p',46,60)]:
        with (base/f'test9_{sec}.tsv').open('w',newline='') as f:
            w=csv.writer(f,delimiter='\t',lineterminator='\n')
            w.writerows(r for r in rows if lo<=r[0]<=hi)
